// Chapter 21 — Output targets.
//
// Written against #213, the wave-5 pass over the *stripped* crates. Two
// things changed under this chapter and both are stated where they belong:
// there are three backends now (`:skill` went with the skill target, #211),
// and `wcl wdoc serve` lost sub-site scoping with `include` (#210) — a
// rebuild request drains every pending change and hands the whole set to
// `build_incremental`.
//
// Every transcript, output tree and Markdown fragment below was captured
// from a real build before it was written down.
page wdoc_outputs {
  title = "Output targets"

  h1 "Output targets"

  p "One document, three outputs. `wcl wdoc build` renders a website, `wcl wdoc pdf` a paginated PDF, and `wcl wdoc markdown` a folder of `.md` files. `wcl wdoc serve` is the first of those with a watcher and a browser attached, not a fourth thing."

  p "This chapter covers each target: what it writes, what it can carry, and what it cannot. It then covers the mechanism that keeps the three honest — one closed content vocabulary that every backend must match exhaustively — and the two dev-loop devices built on it, `wcl wdoc serve` and the `markdown_source` block."

  p "[The CLI](lang_cli) lists every flag. This chapter is about what happens after you type them."

  h2 "One document, three renderers"

  p "The three targets are not three exporters bolted onto an HTML generator. Each one reads the same evaluated document and the same **content vocabulary**, and each decides for itself what a heading, a code listing or a callout looks like."

  p "A block reaches a backend by one of two routes. Most blocks **lower**: a WCL function on the block's type returns a node of the content vocabulary — a `Heading`, a `Paragraph`, a `Code`, a `Callout` — and the three renderers each read that node. A minority are **native**: wdoc renders them in Rust, because their output is not expressible in WCL. [Writing your own blocks](wdoc_extending) covers both routes."

  p "The consequence for this chapter is the useful one. A block that lowers reaches all three targets by construction, and the only blocks that can be missing from a target are the natives that say so. [Visibility](wdoc_visibility) covers what the build does about that."

  callout "A new content node is a compile error in three places" {
    class = ["info"]
    body  = "The content vocabulary is a closed union with no generic container and no raw-markup escape hatch. Every backend matches it **exhaustively** — no catch-all arm in the HTML walker, the PDF walker or the Markdown walker. So adding a variant does not silently render as nothing in two of three outputs; it fails to compile until all three say what to do with it. That is the mechanism, not a convention someone remembers to follow."
  }

  h2 "wcl wdoc build — the website"

  p "`build` renders every page to HTML and writes the shared assets beside them."

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl wdoc build main.wcl --out _site
wrote 2 pages
TEXT
  }

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
_site/
  index.html          # a copy of the site's start page
  intro.html
  ref.html
  _wdoc/              # fonts, favicon, icon sprite, page manifest, player scripts
TEXT
  }

  p "`_wdoc/` holds everything the pages share, and every page references it by a plain relative path. That is what makes an output tree **relocatable**: move `_site/` anywhere, serve it from any prefix, and the links still resolve. [Documents, pages and sites](wdoc_sites) covers the multi-site layouts and what `--site` does to them."

  h3 "build never wipes --out"

  p "The output directory is created if missing and written into. It is never emptied first."

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl wdoc build main.wcl --out _site
wrote 1 page
$ touch _site/leftover.html
$ wcl wdoc build main.wcl --out _site
wrote 1 page
$ ls _site
index.html
leftover.html
one.html
_wdoc
TEXT
  }

  p "`leftover.html` is still there. This cuts both ways and you should know which way you want. It means a build can write into a directory that already holds hand-authored files, and it means a **renamed or deleted page leaves its old file behind**. Delete the directory yourself when you want a clean tree — that is what the book's own build script does before it runs."

  callout "Everything the site needs is served, not read from disk" {
    class = ["warning"]
    body  = "Search fetches its index over HTTP, and so do the player scripts. The site works when a host serves it, or when `wcl wdoc serve` does. Opening a page directly from disk gives you the prose and loses the search box."
  }

  h2 "wcl wdoc serve — the dev loop"

  p "`serve` runs a build, then serves the result over HTTP with a watcher and a live-reload script in every page."

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl wdoc serve main.wcl --out _serve
rendered 2 pages
serving http://127.0.0.1:8199  (source: main.wcl, out: _serve)
auto-rebuild is off — press Enter here to rebuild after edits
TEXT
  }

  p "That third line is the design, not a missing feature. The watcher sees every `.wcl` change and **accumulates** it. It does not rebuild:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
2 file changes pending — press Enter to rebuild
TEXT
  }

  p "A rebuild happens when you ask for one, and there are exactly two ways to ask. Press **Enter** in the console where `serve` is running, or send `POST /__wdoc_rebuild`:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8199/__wdoc_rebuild
{"ok":true,"summary":"2 pages (intro, ref)"}
TEXT
  }

  p "The HTTP form **waits** for the build and reports what it did, so an editor hook or a file-watcher of your own can trigger a rebuild and know whether it succeeded. The browser reloads on its own: each page long-polls `/__wdoc_reload` and reloads when the build generation changes. The generation moves on a failed build too, so a browser parked on the error page picks up the fix."

  p "Omitting `--out` gives you a temporary directory that is removed on shutdown, which is usually what you want while writing."

  h3 "What a rebuild re-renders"

  p "A rebuild drains **every** pending change at once and hands the whole set to the incremental builder. There is no scoping below that: it is the changed set, or a full build."

  p "The incremental builder maps each changed file onto the top-level blocks that came from it. When every changed file contributed only `page` blocks, it re-renders those pages in place and leaves the shared site-wide artifacts — the icon sprite, the search index, the CSS embedded in each page — untouched:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
rebuilt: 2 pages (intro, ref)
TEXT
  }

  p "Anything else falls back to a full rebuild, and says so:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
rebuilt: 2 pages (full)
TEXT
  }

  p "The fallback is deliberately eager, because the failure mode of being clever here is a stale page that looks right. A change to an imported library, to the page set, to the CSS, to an asset declaration, to a repeater, or one that pulls in an icon the sprite does not have yet, all force a full build. So does a change to a file that holds a `site` block — which is why editing your entry document usually rebuilds everything."

  callout "Scoping is by file, not by block" {
    class = ["note"]
    body  = "Two pages in one `pages.wcl` are one unit: edit either and both re-render. That is not a limitation worth working around by splitting files. The saving is in skipping the *other* fifty pages and the aggregate writes, and the parse happens either way because imports force it."
  }

  h2 "wcl wdoc pdf — print"

  p "`pdf` renders each site to one paginated PDF. It is pure Rust: no browser, no headless Chrome, no external binary."

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl wdoc pdf main.wcl --out _pdf
wrote 1 pdf
$ ls _pdf
handbook.pdf
TEXT
  }

  p "The file is named after the **site**, not after the source document. With no `site` block at all, the source file stem names it. `--page-size letter` switches from A4."

  p "Prose, headings, lists, tables, code listings, callouts, footnotes, chapter headers, images and maths all paginate. Diagrams, sequence and state diagrams, terminals and wireframes are painted from the same shape vocabulary the other targets draw, so a diagram in a PDF is the same drawing at the same viewBox — not a screenshot and not a fallback."

  p "What a PDF cannot do is anything interactive or anything that lives beside the document:"

  list {
    li "**Video** shows its poster image, with a link only when the source is an online URL. A link to a local path in a distributed PDF is worse than no link."
    li "**`file`** is not covered at all. There is no output folder beside a PDF to ship a copy into, so a `file` on a page you build to PDF is refused until you waive it with `@except(backends = [:pdf])`. [Visibility](wdoc_visibility) covers the waiver."
    li "**Search, the theme toggle, diagram pan-and-zoom, terminal replay and the deck player** are HTML devices. They are simply absent."
  }

  h2 "wcl wdoc markdown — text"

  p "`markdown` writes one `.md` per page, plus the standalone `.svg` files that Markdown references. `wcl wdoc md` is an alias."

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl wdoc markdown main.wcl --out _md
wrote 1 page
$ find _md -type f
_md/index.md
_md/one.md
_md/_wdoc/one-diagram-1.svg
_md/_wdoc/video-clip-b97542c2.mp4
TEXT
  }

  p "The target is aimed at readers that want text: a model, a search index, a repository someone browses on a code host. So it keeps meaning and drops chrome."

  p "Here is a page carrying frontmatter, prose, an equation, a diagram and a video, and the whole file it produced:"

  code markdown {
    filename = "_md/one.md"
    source = <<'MD'
---
author: platform
status: draft
---

# One

Prose with **bold**, `code` and a [link](one.md).

$$
E = mc^2
$$

![diagram](_wdoc/one-diagram-1.svg)

[_wdoc/video-clip-b97542c2.mp4](_wdoc/video-clip-b97542c2.mp4)
MD
  }

  p "Read the five conversions out of that:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "In the document"        | "In the Markdown" |
      | "a `frontmatter` block"  | "a YAML header — the other two targets pass over it" |
      | "`[link](one)`"          | "`[link](one.md)` — a page name becomes a file name" |
      | "a `math` block"         | "`$$ … $$`, still LaTeX, for the reader to typeset" |
      | "a `diagram`"            | "a standalone `.svg` under `_wdoc/`, referenced by an image" |
      | "a `video`"              | "a link; a local source is copied out first" |
  }

  p "Two more worth knowing. A `code` block becomes a fence under its filename rather than a styled card. A `callout` becomes a GitHub alert (`> [!NOTE]`), keyed on the callout's kind:"

  code markdown {
    source = <<'MD'
> [!NOTE]
> **One source**
>
> Every target reads the same document.
MD
  }

  p "Diagrams, terminals and wireframes render as **plain SVG** rather than as an approximation in text, which is why a zoomable diagram survives the trip. Equations stay LaTeX for the same reason: converting either to ASCII would lose the thing worth keeping."

  h2 "markdown_source — previewing the Markdown"

  p "There is a block for reading a page's generated Markdown _inside_ the book, next to the rendered version. Wrap the content in `markdown_source`:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
markdown_source {
  id = "pv"
  h2 "A section"
  p "Prose with **bold** and `code`."
  list {
    li "one"
    li "two"
  }
}
WCL
  }

  p "The HTML build lowers the body through the Markdown emitter and shows the result as a highlighted `code markdown` listing:"

  code markdown {
    source = <<'MD'
## A section

Prose with **bold** and `code`.

- one
- two
MD
  }

  p "Set `id` to the previewed page's name. It is the filename stem for any diagram or terminal SVGs the body's Markdown writes, so the `![](…)` references line up with what a real `wcl wdoc markdown` run would produce."

  p "The block is native on `:html` and only `:html`, because it taps the Markdown emitter from inside the HTML build. Using it on another target is refused rather than rendering nothing — see [Visibility](wdoc_visibility) for the waiver."

  h2 "Choosing a target"

  p "The three are not a quality ladder. Each answers a different question."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Target"     | "Reach for it when" | "Give up" |
      | "`build`"    | "People will read it in a browser" | "nothing — this is the full-fidelity target" |
      | "`pdf`"      | "It has to print, or travel as one file" | "interactivity, `file` assets, playable video" |
      | "`markdown`" | "A model, a diff or a code host will read it" | "layout, theming, search; keeps the meaning" |
  }

  p "Building more than one from the same source is the normal case, and it is what the `backends` axis is for: the paragraph that only makes sense in a browser carries `@only(backends = [:html])`, and everything else is written once."

  h2 "Where to go next"

  list {
    li "[The CLI](lang_cli) — every flag of `build`, `serve`, `pdf` and `markdown`, and the exit codes they return."
    li "[Visibility](wdoc_visibility) — `@only` and `@except`, and waiving a native block on a target that cannot render it."
    li "[Documents, pages and sites](wdoc_sites) — the output tree, multi-site routing, the start page and search."
    li "[Writing your own blocks](wdoc_extending) — lowering versus `@native`, and how a block declares the targets it covers."
    li "[Code](wdoc_code) — the chrome each backend draws around a `code` block."
    li "[Images, videos and file assets](wdoc_media) — the `file` block the PDF target does not cover."
  }
}
